        "topic": "Unknown Topic",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "question": "A 40 year old male undergoes resection of the upper small intestine due to trauma. One year later he develops fatigue, oral ulcers, and breathlessness. CBC shows MCV 110 fL. What is the most likely deficiency?",
        "options": {
            "A": "Folate deficiency",
            "B": "Vitamin B6 deficiency",
            "C": "Iron deficiency",
            "D": "Vitamin B12 deficiency",
            "E": ""
        },
        "correctAnswer": "A",
        "explanation": "<p><h3 style=\"color: black;\">Correct Answer:</h3></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #008000;\">Folate deficiency</strong> - Folate is absorbed mainly in the <strong>proximal small intestine, especially the jejunum</strong>. Resection of the upper small intestine can therefore cause folate deficiency, leading to <strong>megaloblastic macrocytic anemia</strong> with an increased MCV of 110 fL.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: black;\">How to Pick the Answer:</h3></p><p><p>The key clue is <strong>upper small intestine resection</strong> combined with <strong>MCV 110 fL</strong>. High MCV indicates megaloblastic anemia, pointing toward folate or vitamin B12 deficiency. Since folate is absorbed in the <strong>upper/proximal small intestine</strong>, folate deficiency is the best answer.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: black;\">Wrong Answers</h3></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #CC0000;\">Vitamin B6 deficiency</strong> - B6 deficiency can cause sideroblastic/microcytic anemia rather than megaloblastic macrocytic anemia.</p></p><p><br></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #CC0000;\">Iron deficiency</strong> - Causes <strong>microcytic hypochromic anemia</strong> with a low MCV.</p></p><p><br></p><p><p><strong style=\"color: #CC0000;\">Vitamin B12 deficiency</strong> - Also causes megaloblastic macrocytic anemia, but B12 is absorbed mainly in the <strong>terminal ileum</strong> after binding intrinsic factor. Upper small-intestinal resection therefore does not primarily impair B12 absorption.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: #0066CC;\">Key Concept:</h3></p><p><p><strong>Folate → proximal small intestine</strong><br></p><p><strong>Vitamin B12 → terminal ileum</strong></p></p><p><p>Both deficiencies cause <strong>megaloblastic anemia → increased MCV</strong>.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: #8A2BE2;\">Mnemonic:</h3></p><p><p><strong>Folate = Front</strong> → absorbed in the proximal/upper small intestine.<br></p><p><strong>B12 = Bottom</strong> → absorbed in the terminal ileum.</p></p><p><br></p><p><h3 style=\"color: #CC6600;\">Exam Trap:</h3></p><p><p>Do not choose B12 just because the MCV is high. First identify the <strong>site of intestinal resection</strong>: <strong>upper/proximal small intestine → folate</strong>; <strong>terminal ileum → B12</strong>.</p></p>",
        "hint": "<p><br></p>"
    },
